course: dictionary
lesson: 5101
title: Internal: inside or within
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Internal means inside something. You can use it for the inside of a body, a building or a country, and for things that happen within a group, not with outsiders. Look at the words around it to see which meaning fits.
explain: In science, your «internal organs», such as your heart and lungs, are inside your body. In maths, a shape's internal angles are the angles on the inside of the shape. A triangle's internal angles always add up to 180°.
explain: At school, an «internal exam» is one set by your own school for its own pupils, not by outsiders. In everyday life, internal doors are inside a building, and an internal phone works within one office or organisation.
explain: Rhymes can also sit inside a line. «Rhymes can come at the ends of lines or inside a line.» An internal rhyme has its rhyming words inside one line, as in «Hey diddle diddle, the cat and the fiddle». To check your meaning, swap in "inside" and see if the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Internal means inside, or happening within a group or organisation.
fact: «Internal organs» such as the heart and lungs are inside the body.
fact: «Internal angles» are the angles inside a shape, and a triangle's internal angles add up to 180°.
fact: «An internal exam» is set by your own school for its own pupils, not by outsiders.
fact: «Internal rhyme» has the rhyming words inside one line, as in «Hey diddle diddle, the cat and the fiddle».
exercise: The doctor checked the patient's «internal organs», such as the heart and lungs. Where are these organs? | On the skin | Under the desk | Outside the building | *Inside the body
exercise: In maths, what are the internal angles of a triangle? | Angles that add up to 90° | Angles on the outside of the shape | *Angles on the inside of the shape, which add up to 180° | Sides that are all the same length
exercise: Ruby's class sat «an internal exam» set by their school. Who set it? | *Their own school, for its own pupils | A visiting film crew | An outside examiner from another country | The local newspaper
test: «The internal doors were painted blue.» What does internal mean here? | Outside, in the garden | Made of glass | *Inside the building | Very loud
test: What is an internal rhyme? | *Rhyming words that sit inside a line | A rhyme only at the very end of a line | A poem with no rhymes at all | A rhyme that is sung very loudly
exam: Which sentence uses internal correctly? | *The doctor checked her internal organs | I walked internal to the shop | We ate internal for lunch | The internal rain fell on the hill
